good morning shadow chancellor um  i'm going to come to you on the things  i've been discussing with the education  section in a second but uh you said this  morning that your big plan to deal with  this cost of living crisis is a windfall  tax on north sea oil and gas producers  how's that going to work in a declining  industry uh  and one which basically isn't producing  much in the way of profits at the moment  anyway  well we've had 10 years more than a  decade of dither and delay and  incompetence from this government which  is why we have this energy price crisis  today of course prices have gone up  globally but we are uniquely exposed in  britain because of the decisions of this  government it's a failure to regulate  properly to get rid of our gas storage  and a failure to retrofit homes and to  go with the speed on new nuclear and  renewables that are necessary and that's  why today i'm putting up forward a  package of measures to keep bills down  this april to help families and  pensioners who are struggling with this  cost of living crisis that is coming in  april and it is right to ask those who  have benefited from high higher gas and  oil prices to pay more into the system  and that's why i'm putting forward a  one-off increase in the taxes paid by  north sea oil and gas they are likely to  make record profits uh this year because  of that spike in prices let's tax them a  bit more and use that money to reduce  bills for ordinary people who are  struggling so much at the moment and we  estimate that um just because of the  higher  prices already the oil and gas industry  in the north sea are likely to pay 2.3  billion pounds more in tax a one-off  windfall tax will raise an additional  1.2 billion pounds let's put that money  into keeping bills lower for everybody  else  okay with respect to senator chancellor  um  it's okay for you to say that they're  having a terrific time uh because of  this the temporary spike in oil price uh  oil prices but from those in the  industry who actually have to pay the  bills they are saying that hasn't  particularly helped them and actually  things are going pretty much south so  how do you respond to their view that  actually  it may help somebody but it certainly  isn't helping them and that what you  might be doing is preparing for the  decimation  of our energy sector  well chancellors have used this device  before of increasing taxes on north sea  oil and gas when revenues and profits  are particularly high the previous  chancellor george osborne did it in 2011  gordon brown and before him margaret  thatcher also increased taxes for a  short period of time on north sea oil  and gas when prices were particularly  high and those windfall profits were  being made at any chancellor any shadow  chancellor has to make choices about  where to tax and where to spend i want  to tax fairly and spend wisely but when  those excess profits are being made it  does make sense to have a windfall tax  and use that money to keep bills down  for everybody else because unless action  is taken and taken soon  millions of families and pensioners  across the country  are staring into a huge cost of living  crisis as you said earlier trevor an  increase in national insurance at the  same time that gas and electricity bills  are going to explode potentially up by  700 pounds a year for bill payers  i we cannot stand by as the government  are currently doing and allowing those  price increases to go ahead when action  can be taken now to reduce bills  especially for those who are the squeeze  middle on lower incomes of pensioner  families uh as well action has to be  taken taken to avert this crisis that is  coming down the line okay just one more  chance on this i'm not going to argue  with you about the nature of the problem  i'm talking i'm asking about the nature  of the solution um  you are  projecting a windfall tax on a declining  industry reserves are declining which  already says it's squeezed and actually  they um  that though the big oil companies are  going elsewhere to develop are you are  you happy to uh project a plan which  essentially will uh  will  make britain more dependent on energy  supplies from others for example uh mr  putin's got lots of oil and gas uh are  you happy that we are  making him smile this morning by saying  we might have to depend more on him  plan my plan trevor is the exact  opposite we need to wean ourself off  imported gas from russia and qatar and  elsewhere and that's why in the plan  that i'm setting out today is also a big  boost in investment in renewables and in  nuclear in our hydrogen industry so we  are not reliant that is why the uk is  uniquely exposed because the government  got rid of the gas reserves has dithered  and delayed for over a decade on new  nuclear and in investment in renewables  and as a result we are particularly  exposed to that imported gas from  countries like russia who do have us  over a barrel and that is why as well as  a short-term action to keep prices down  we've got to retrofit homes speed up  that investment in new nuclear and  renewables and ensure that we are never  in this position again because i said as  i say the uk is uniquely exposed to  these rising gas prices because of a  failure for over a decade now by this  government to take action and that's why  kia stammer and myself working with ed  miliband our shadow secretary of state  for climate and net zero have put  forward this balanced package of  measures to keep prices low by asking  those who are making big profits this  year to pay more into the system for  this one year only  forgive me i i know it's it's it's a bit  tricky because of the technicalities to  dave uh that you can't him hear me  interrupting but forgive me interrupting  you but the other part of your plan is  to cut vat on uh energy  bills um that's a bit odd for a labor  shadow chancellor isn't it because that  would be uh benefit richer households  relative to poorer households why not  just simply uh go straight to the tax  system i mean that would be a  progressive step rather than a  regressive way of dealing with the  problem  i'm setting out today three things to  keep bills low first of all a cut in vat  on gas and electricity bills from five  percent to zero for one year uh saving  uh households given the price rise we're  expected to see a hundred pounds a year  the second thing is smoothing the some  of the costs of the increase that we're  likely to see in april to take a further  100 pounds of bills but recognizing that  it is those on lower incomes families  and pensioners as well as well as those  who uh the squeezed middle uh who are  struggling with those rising bills and  rising tax putting extra support through  the warms homes discount to ensure that  those people get the help that is needed  an extra 400 pounds off their bills from  april uh this year to help them through  this difficult time with the prices high  and you'll know trevor that the prime  minister was the biggest advocate for  cutting vat on gas and electricity bills  during the european referendum but now  when cutting those bills would make more  difference than ever the prime minister  says no well i say that bills can't be  paid on broken promises the government  should honor that commitment that the  prime minister made to take vat of gas  and electricity prices if this isn't the  time to do it then frankly i don't know  when is  it it's a bit odd um rachel ruiz to to  hear you saying uh you want to follow a  boris johnson policy and i don't think  you've quite dealt with my question  about whether this is not uh a  regressive uh tax on households and  wouldn't a labor approach be to  uh impose a more progressive tax so that  you're not putting a relatively higher  burden on the low paid you can do all  the other things that you've mentioned i  hear all that but this particular  cut would mean that better off  households benefit relative to the poor  why are you doing that  well in fact it is the poorer households  who spend a higher proportion of their  spending on gas and electricity bills  and that is particularly so for  pensioner households who are at home  more and need to keep their homes warm  so i don't buy this um idea that cutting  vit is somehow a regressive policy but  we are making sure that through this  package of measures we're announcing  today and the windfall tax on those  excess profits of north sea oil and gas  are used particularly to benefit those  people on lower incomes families and  pensioners with additional support for  the warm homes discount so support for  all with that cut in v80 but extra  support for those who need it most that  is the progressive thing to do but it  also recognizes that everybody right now  is facing through no fault of their own  big increases in prices in april  government can do something about it  they must choose to act because without  action a combination of national  insurance increases council tax  increases and this catastrophe of higher  domestic gas and electricity bills will  be a disaster for families and  pensioners in just a couple of months  time  rachel reese thank you very much for  your time this morning
 

